# Arabic RTL Web

Use this rule when building or fixing Arabic web interfaces that should feel natively RTL. Covers everything from plain HTML/CSS to Tailwind, React, Next.js, Vue, Nuxt, and static sites.

## Supported Stacks

- plain HTML/CSS
- Tailwind CSS (v3 and v4)
- React and Next.js (App Router and Pages Router)
- Vue and Nuxt
- any CSS-in-JS library (styled-components, emotion, etc.)

## Core Rule

Before fixing individual components, determine whether the page already has a working RTL context.

Check for `dir="rtl"` on `<html>` or a parent container. If the root is already RTL, the browser's layout engine will handle most mirroring automatically. Do not add redundant direction overrides on child elements.

Before fixing rows, icons, or controls, run a visual sentinel check.

### Visual Sentinel Check

Place a full-width Arabic text block on the page:

```html
<p style="width: 100%; text-align: start;">اختبار الاتجاه</p>
```

Inspect visually:
- if the text sits on the visual right edge, the page is behaving as RTL. continue.
- if it sits on the visual left edge, fix the root direction first.

Do not begin component-level fixes until this passes.

## Setting Root Direction

### HTML

```html
<html lang="ar" dir="rtl">
```

This is the single most important line. Most CSS logical properties, flexbox, and grid will automatically respect this.

### Next.js App Router

In `layout.tsx`:

```tsx
export default function RootLayout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <html lang="ar" dir="rtl">
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  )
}
```

For bilingual sites that toggle language, make `dir` dynamic:

```tsx
<html lang={locale} dir={locale === 'ar' ? 'rtl' : 'ltr'}>
```

### Next.js Pages Router

In `_document.tsx`, set `dir` on `<Html>`. Or set it dynamically in `_app.tsx` via `useEffect` on `document.documentElement`.

### Vue / Nuxt

In Nuxt, use `useHead()` to set `htmlAttrs: { dir: 'rtl', lang: 'ar' }`.

## CSS Logical Properties

The most common RTL mistake on the web is using physical direction properties (`left`, `right`, `margin-left`, `padding-right`) instead of logical ones.

Logical properties automatically flip in RTL context:

| physical (avoid)     | logical (prefer)         |
|---------------------|--------------------------|
| `margin-left`       | `margin-inline-start`    |
| `margin-right`      | `margin-inline-end`      |
| `padding-left`      | `padding-inline-start`   |
| `padding-right`     | `padding-inline-end`     |
| `left`              | `inset-inline-start`     |
| `right`             | `inset-inline-end`       |
| `text-align: left`  | `text-align: start`      |
| `text-align: right` | `text-align: end`        |
| `border-left`       | `border-inline-start`    |
| `border-right`      | `border-inline-end`      |
| `float: left`       | `float: inline-start`    |
| `float: right`      | `float: inline-end`      |

Use logical properties everywhere. Physical properties should only appear when you intentionally want something pinned to a specific visual side regardless of text direction.

## Tailwind CSS

Tailwind v3+ supports RTL with `rtl:` and `ltr:` variants and logical properties.

### Logical spacing and positioning

Use `ms-` (margin-start) and `me-` (margin-end) instead of `ml-` and `mr-`:

```html
<!-- avoid -->
<div class="ml-4 pr-2">

<!-- prefer -->
<div class="ms-4 pe-2">
```

Full mapping:
- `ms-*` = margin-inline-start (replaces `ml-*` in LTR)
- `me-*` = margin-inline-end (replaces `mr-*` in LTR)
- `ps-*` = padding-inline-start
- `pe-*` = padding-inline-end
- `start-*` = inset-inline-start (replaces `left-*`)
- `end-*` = inset-inline-end (replaces `right-*`)

### Directional variants

For cases where you need different behavior per direction:

```html
<div class="ltr:ml-4 rtl:mr-4">
```

But prefer logical properties (`ms-4`) over directional variants when possible. Variants are for edge cases.

## Flexbox and Grid

Flexbox and CSS Grid automatically respect `dir="rtl"`. A `flex-row` container will place its first child on the right in RTL.

Do not use `flex-row-reverse` to "fix" RTL layout if the root already has `dir="rtl"`. The browser handles it.

Exception: if you are building a component that must always render in a specific visual order regardless of language (like a media player's controls), use explicit ordering or a local `dir="ltr"` override.

## Arabic Text Blocks

For Arabic headings, descriptions, and body copy:

- give the text block full available width
- use `text-align: start` (not `text-align: right`)
- do not let Arabic text shrink to intrinsic width if the design expects it to fill a row or card

```html
<div class="w-full text-start">
  <h2>عنوان الصفحة</h2>
  <p>وصف تفصيلي للمحتوى</p>
</div>
```

## Arabic Row Composition

An Arabic row has two zones:

- main text content on the visual right
- secondary control or accessory on the visual left

With `dir="rtl"` on the root, a simple `flex` row handles this naturally:

```html
<div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4">
  <div class="flex-1 min-w-0">
    <h3>عنوان العنصر</h3>
    <p>وصف مختصر</p>
  </div>
  <span class="shrink-0">badge or control</span>
</div>
```

Because the root is RTL, the text block lands on the right and the accessory on the left. No `flex-row-reverse` needed.

## Arabic Typography

### Font Loading

Common Arabic web fonts: IBM Plex Sans Arabic, Noto Sans Arabic, Cairo, Tajawal, Almarai (all on Google Fonts).

For Next.js with `next/font`:

```tsx
import { IBM_Plex_Sans_Arabic } from 'next/font/google'

const arabic = IBM_Plex_Sans_Arabic({
  subsets: ['arabic'],
  weight: ['400', '500', '600', '700'],
  variable: '--font-arabic',
})
```

### Line Height and Spacing

Arabic script is taller than Latin. Use `line-height` 1.7 to 1.8 for body text. Arabic words are often longer than English. Allow text wrapping and avoid fixed widths on text containers.

### Bilingual Font Stacks

```css
font-family: 'IBM Plex Sans Arabic', 'Space Grotesk', system-ui, sans-serif;
```

The browser picks the Arabic font for Arabic characters and falls through to Latin fonts for English.

## Navigation and Icons

### Back/Forward Arrows

In Arabic contexts: back arrow points right, forward arrow points left.

```html
<ChevronRight class="rtl:rotate-180" />
```

Or use CSS:

```css
[dir="rtl"] .icon-back {
  transform: scaleX(-1);
}
```

### Navigation Menus

Horizontal navs automatically reorder in RTL with flexbox. Verify logo is on visual right, nav links flow right to left, dropdowns open correctly.

## Forms

Form labels should appear above or to the right of inputs in RTL. Placeholder text should be right-aligned. Position validation messages using `inset-inline-end` not `right`.

## Tables

Tables automatically respect RTL. First column appears on the right. For numeric columns that should stay LTR (phone numbers, currency codes), wrap in `<span dir="ltr">`.

## Animations and Transitions

Slide-in animations should come from the correct direction. Use logical properties or swap animation direction:

```css
[dir="rtl"] .slide-in { animation-name: slide-in-right; }
[dir="ltr"] .slide-in { animation-name: slide-in-left; }
```

## Bilingual Site Patterns

- store locale in cookie or URL path, toggle `dir` on `<html>`
- keep content in separate locale files or CMS
- build components with logical properties only (should work in both directions without changes)
- use `hreflang` tags, set `lang` correctly, Arabic pages need Arabic meta descriptions

## Common Failure Modes

- `ml-*` / `mr-*` in Tailwind instead of `ms-*` / `me-*`
- `left` / `right` in CSS instead of logical properties
- `text-align: right` instead of `text-align: start`
- `flex-row-reverse` when root is already RTL
- `dir="rtl"` on every element instead of just root
- Arabic text shrinking to intrinsic width
- directional icons not flipping
- hardcoded `left: 0` / `right: 0` instead of `inset-inline-start` / `inset-inline-end`
- mixed content where numbers or English break visual flow (use `<span dir="ltr">` or `unicode-bidi: isolate`)

## Visual QA Checklist

After implementing RTL, verify on every page:
- `<html>` has `dir="rtl"` and `lang="ar"`
- sentinel text lands on visual right
- navigation flows right to left
- back arrows point right, disclosure chevrons point left
- form labels and placeholder text are right-aligned
- cards have text on visual right, accessories on visual left
- no physical `left/right` CSS remains (except intentional)
- icons with directional meaning are flipped
- animations slide from correct side
- numbers and English text wrapped with `dir="ltr"` where needed
- fonts support Arabic glyphs
- line height is comfortable for Arabic text

## Working Style

1. identify the stack
2. check if root direction is already set
3. run visual sentinel check
4. fix text block width and logical alignment first
5. fix row composition second
6. fix icons, arrows, and directional elements third
7. run visual QA checklist on every affected page
